The caller half of the fix for issue #130, parked here because this
repository's agent cannot push it.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Agents run as `claude[bot]` through the Claude
GitHub App -- `ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN` is deliberately unset, and
CLAUDE_AUTOMATION.md says so. That installation token has no `workflows`
permission, so a push touching `.github/workflows/**` is refused by
GitHub itself before any review sees it:

    ! [remote rejected] claude/codex-answer-provenance-130
      (refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow
       `.github/workflows/ci.yml` without `workflows` permission)

Everything a token may write is in the branch. These two files are the
rest, and until they land the rule in .github/scripts/codex-answered.sh
is present, tested and NOT CALLED -- which is why
.github/tests/merge-candidate-test.sh, the suite CI already runs, fails
two assertions naming exactly that. CI is red on purpose until this is
applied.

APPLY IT, from an account that holds `workflows` (the owner's, or an
agent given a fine-grained PAT as `ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN`):

    git checkout claude/codex-answer-provenance-130
    git apply docs/automation/pending/130-merge-sweep-caller.patch
    git rm -r docs/automation/pending
    git commit -am "The merge sweep asks the rule that has a test"
    git push

CI then goes green: merge-candidate-test.sh 86/86 and the new
codex-answered-test.sh 61/61, which the ci.yml half of this patch wires
in.

WHAT IT CHANGES. pr-merge-sweep.yml gathers inline review comments as a
third source (they are the only shape carrying `in_reply_to_id`), looks
each distinct non-bot commenter's permission up through
`repos/{repo}/collaborators/{login}/permission`, and hands the lot to
codex-answered.sh instead of deciding in four lines of `jq`. ci.yml gains
one step running that rule's test.

